     1	# Runtime Messages
     2	
     3	Use deterministic, bounded v1 JSON models to exchange non-secret configuration
     4	references and immutable runtime state without importing platform or transport
     5	types into `ReluxTunnelCore`.
     6	
     7	## Wire families
     8	
     9	The legacy `version` request and response remain an exact exception with only
    10	`kind` and `protocolVersion`. Every M1 app message has required
    11	`protocolVersion`, `kind`, and `schemaVersion` fields. `requestID` is the only
    12	optional common field and defaults to absent. The system-owned provider
    13	configuration snapshot and start request are not app messages, but both remain
    14	versioned, kind-bearing wire objects.
    15	
    16	| Model | Version | Maximum JSON bytes | Required payload fields | Defaults |
    17	| --- | --- | ---: | --- | --- |
    18	| `ProviderVersionRequest`, `ProviderVersionResponse` | protocol 1 | 4 KiB | `protocolVersion`, `kind=version` | request protocol version 1 when constructed |
    19	| `TunnelConfigurationReference` | schema 1 | 4 KiB | `schemaVersion`, `profileIdentifier` | current schema when constructed |
    20	| `RuntimeStartRequest` | protocol 1, schema 1 | 4 KiB | `protocolVersion`, `schemaVersion`, `kind=sshProfileSnapshotStart`, `configurationGeneration`, `snapshotDigestSHA256` | current protocol and schema when constructed |
    21	| `RuntimeConfigurationSnapshot` | protocol 1, schema 1 | 64 KiB | generation, profile identity/revision, credential reference, trust reference, compatible route mode | absent `requestID` |
    22	| `RuntimeCommand` | protocol 1, schema 1 | 4 KiB | one read-only command kind | absent `requestID` |
    23	| `RuntimeProtocolCapabilitiesSnapshot` | protocol 1, schema 1 | 4 KiB | protocol range and per-kind schema ranges | current protocol range; absent `requestID` |
    24	| `RuntimeCapabilitySnapshot` | protocol 1, schema 1 | 16 KiB | generation, sequence, TCP, safe DNS, UDP, route mode, route-installed, and health facts | absent `requestID`; capability facts never default while decoding |
    25	| `RuntimeLifecycleSnapshot` | protocol 1, schema 1 | 16 KiB | generation, sequence, lifecycle/route state, and all capability facts | absent `requestID` and `error` |
    26	| `RuntimeDiagnosticsSnapshot` | protocol 1, schema 1 | 64 KiB | generation and sequence | absent `requestID`; empty aggregate collections |
    27	| `RuntimeProtocolError` | protocol 1, schema 1 | 4 KiB | finite error code and supported protocol/schema ranges | current supported ranges; absent `requestID` |
    28	
    29	All opaque identifiers encode as UUID strings. Configuration models have no
    30	arbitrary byte fields, free-form parameter dictionary, private-key field, or
    31	passphrase field. Credential and trust values are lookup references only.
    32	
    33	## Compatibility and rejection
    34	
    35	`RuntimeMessageCodec` and `RuntimeConfigurationCodec` emit sorted-key JSON and
    36	enforce the model-specific encoded limit in both directions. Decoding rejects
    37	invalid UTF-8, non-object roots, trailing bytes, duplicate keys (including
    38	escape-equivalent keys), nesting deeper than 16 levels, corrupt JSON numbers,
    39	missing required fields, unsupported protocol/schema versions, unknown input
    40	kinds, and unknown provider-input values through `RuntimeMessageCodecError`.
    41	The start-request decoder accepts exactly five keys: `protocolVersion`,
    42	`schemaVersion`, `kind`, `configurationGeneration`, and
    43	`snapshotDigestSHA256`. Missing or additional keys are corrupt input. It also
    44	rejects unsupported protocol or schema versions, a kind other than
    45	`sshProfileSnapshotStart`, a zero generation, and a digest that is not exactly
    46	64 lowercase hexadecimal characters.
    47	
    48	Before the first immutable runtime capture, a present start request must match
    49	both the configuration generation and the SHA-256 digest of the exact stored
    50	canonical snapshot bytes. Either mismatch returns
    51	`profileGenerationMismatch`; matching only the generation is insufficient and
    52	therefore rejects same-generation field replacement. The contract also permits
    53	nil start options, in which case the loader may capture the complete validated
    54	snapshot currently stored in `providerConfiguration`. Subsequent capture calls
    55	must supply the same stored snapshot bytes as the first capture.
    56	
    57	Unknown object fields are ignored and disappear on re-encoding. Unknown output
    58	lifecycle, route-state, or route-mode values project to `unknown` with every
    59	capability false. Known capability fields remain required, so their absence is
    60	corrupt rather than an optimistic default. Diagnostics generation and sequence
    61	remain required while absent aggregate collections decode to their documented
    62	empty defaults. `RuntimeSnapshotPosition.isNewer(than:)` implements the
    63	generation/sequence discard rule.
    64	
    65	The v1 capability schema deliberately has independent `tcp`, `safeDNS`, `udp`,
    66	`routeMode`, `routesInstalled`, and `healthy` fields and no aggregate full-mode
    67	claim. M1 producers publish TCP and safe DNS independently while UDP remains
    68	false.
    69	
    70	## Provider routing and retirement
    71	
    72	`TunnelProviderAdapter` accepts only `getProtocolCapabilities`,
    73	`getRuntimeSnapshot`, `getCapabilities`, and `getDiagnostics` as v1 provider
    74	commands. Although `RuntimeCommand` keeps `requestID` optional for wire-model
    75	compatibility, the provider router requires a UUID for every v1 request. It
    76	serializes reservations per runtime generation, bounds active and recently
    77	completed identifiers, rejects concurrent duplicates, and copies the request
    78	identifier into every successful response or post-decode protocol error.
    79	
    80	Each non-nil Apple response handler is wrapped in a once-only gate. Stop retires
    81	the generation and completes every accepted pending gate without waiting for a
    82	late snapshot or diagnostics callback. Nil handlers are never retained. Source
    83	lookups race the same retirement signal, so their late results cannot update or
    84	retain another provider generation.
    85	
    86	Provider cleanup is one joined operation with an injected ten-second graceful
    87	budget. Cancellation fans out through a fixed-capacity cleanup registry; expiry
    88	force-closes every registered controllable handle and records only a finite
    89	`cleanup_deadline_exceeded` error plus the raw numeric Apple reason. No localized
    90	platform error text, configuration, credential, endpoint, or traffic value is
    91	added to the message or cleanup diagnostics.
    92	
    93	## Host projection
    94	
    95	`VPNSessionController` sends the read-only protocol, lifecycle, and capability
    96	requests only while the freshly read exact `NETunnelProviderSession` status is
    97	connected. Each request has a UUID correlation identifier and a three-second
    98	monotonic deadline. A combined provider projection requires matching lifecycle
    99	and capability positions that are strictly newer than the controller's last
   100	accepted generation/sequence position. Relaunch creates a new controller with
   101	no app-owned runtime history and requests the current facts again.
   102	
   103	System session status remains independent: every non-connected status clears
   104	provider capability immediately, while missing, nil, timed-out, corrupt,
   105	unsupported, wrong-request, mismatched, stale, and late responses leave a
   106	connected system session capability-unknown. Controller retirement removes
   107	observers and cancels host waits without stopping the system tunnel.
